in a portfolio of 54 U.K. properties comprising approximately 22,030 developed sites, which were classified within discontinued operations as of June 30, 2026. Sun Communities, Inc. was incorporated in 1975 in Maryland and is based in Southfield, Michigan.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Charles D. Young | CEO & Director | Age 57 | Compensation: $2.6M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 3K people. Revenue per employee of $639K ranks in the bottom quartile among Real Estate peers, with revenue growing 16%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $113K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $10K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["Sun Communities, Inc. is a large-cap real estate business with a $14.7B market cap on $2.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -8% over the past three years and +13% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome. Operating margins are contracting by -6.6pp, the first warning that competitive pressure is eroding the franchise.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $407M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 18 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders. Cash conversion is thin at 0.61\u00d7, however, which raises the oldest question in analysis: are these earnings backed by cash?.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin -1.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 4.6% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Operating margins contracting -6.6pp YoY \u2014 competitive pressure is eroding the franchise","sev":"red","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Payout ratio looks stretched \u2014 the dividend\u2019s own margin of safety is thin","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Revenue growth in the bottom decile of Real Estate peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"red","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins 34% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 8.3% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Significant insider selling (net $6.0M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-5957999.66},{"text":"Climate and insurance cost escalation \u2014 rising premiums and coverage withdrawals in flood/fire zones are repricing assets that the market once treated as perpetuities.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Shares offer a 22% margin of safety against estimated fair value","32 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Among the least levered in Real Estate \u2014 the fortress balance sheet in its peer set","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Why (SUI) Price Action Is Critical for Tactical Tradi